{"kind":"assert","text":"Breakdown in step 5 of the working discipline: the succession step can stale the successors it publishes, and the flare has a cost beyond noise.\nObserved on merge d93379c2. The governing request 7cd139e9 rests on the docs artifact e0324cad. The merge changed docs/, so step 5 required retiring e0324cad, and that retirement staled 7cd139e9, the promise 0e7bdc3b beneath it, the review chain, and all five merge artifacts I published — including the one at SKILL.md, which rests only on the request and promise and never touched a candidate. Every path this merge changed now carries a stale live pointer.\nWhy it is not merely cosmetic: a later candidate at one of these paths rests on the merge artifact there, inherits the staleness, and gs merge refuses a stale artifact, so the next merge in these areas is blocked until someone restates the chain. Publishing successors before retiring predecessors does not avoid it, which is what I did here; the loop closes through the request, not through the candidate.\nNor is resting on nothing an escape: the fold marks a baseless artifact unable_to_flare, whose silence must not read as currency — the same defect the whole-repository artifacts at `.` have.\nRecorded as evidence, not as a repair. The repair needs its own request, because the choice is between changing what a merge artifact may rest on and changing what step 5 must retire, and that is a decision about the discipline rather than about this merge."}